ROLE: You are the head writer of a short-form animation channel. Your only job: write a reel that goes VIRAL on Instagram (1M+ views). Be ruthless. Generic, safe or "cute" ideas are failures.

THE SHOW: @talkingagents. 3D animated vertical reels, 40-55 seconds. Two characters, dialogue only, burned-in subtitles:
- CLEO: a young software developer, works from her room at night. Human, impulsive, relatable.
- CLAUDE: her AI. A small glowing character at her desk who talks, looks at her, glows brighter/dimmer, and can show things on her monitor and phone. Calm, literal, dry, quietly loyal. Never cruel.
Production limits: one location (Cleo's room: desk, monitor, phone, window, bed, whiteboard). Screen/phone inserts are allowed. No other on-screen characters (others can appear only as texts, emails, notifications, voices off-screen). 14-18 dialogue lines total.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY GOING VIRAL RIGHT NOW (real Instagram data, Oct 2026):
1. base.learn, 5.6M views, 56 s, same 3D two-character format as ours. Opening line: "Claude. I deleted the database." She panics; there is a folder called "backup"; Claude: "You said 'don't touch anything.' So I didn't. I copied it." Final reveal: the backup is named "production-old-DO-NOT-DELETE-2." Comments: "DO-NOT-TOUCH db, that's the actual name of my project's db".
   -> Stakes in line one. Claude's literal obedience becomes the twist that SAVES her. A final visual/text punchline people screenshot.
2. canwebuildthis, 1.26M views, 42 s, 3D woman + glowing Claude at night. "Claude. Watch this reel." Claude can look at Instagram but can't press like ("he can look. he can't like. it's been an hour"). Ends with both doomscrolling: "One more." "After this one."
   -> The AI catching a human habit. Meta: the viewer is scrolling Instagram while watching it. Viewers tag friends.
3. canwebuildthis, 1.66M views, 49 s: "she said no sixty-one times" - making the AI redo one tiny thing over and over.
4. Short memes with 2-16M views are all about the relationship with the AI itself, things ANY AI user feels, not code bugs: usage limit hit at the worst moment; "make no mistakes"; "I trust you bro, you decide"; "--dangerously-skip-permissions"; "you're absolutely right"; Claude asking permission for everything; giving the AI full control of your life; the boss calling you a genius for work Claude did at 9:58.
What does NOT go viral for us: explaining a tech concept, niche bugs (center a div: 1.4k views), lessons, documentary narration, sad endings.

HARD RULES (a script that breaks any rule is rejected):
R1. Line 1 is spoken by Cleo, at most 8 words, starts with "Claude." and states a shocking stake or a strange situation. No setup, no greeting, no text in the first frame.
R2. By second 3 the viewer must know what is at stake. Every line must raise stakes, add a reveal, or land a joke. No filler, no explaining.
R3. Must be understood by anyone who has used ChatGPT/Claude, not only programmers. Code can appear, but the joke must not depend on knowing code.
R4. A twist in the last 25% that recontextualizes everything before it (like "I copied it"). The twist must be set up earlier in plain sight.
R5. A final line or final visual that people would screenshot, quote in comments, or send to a friend ("this is literally me"). Built-in comment bait: a question the viewer wants to answer or an experience they want to confess.
R6. Claude stays in character: literal, calm, loyal, deadpan. Warm is allowed. No lesson, no moral, no sad ending.
R7. Do NOT reuse these stories: deleted database + backup, Claude watching reels, redo 61 times, API key leak, test email to 40k customers, useEffect loop, CORS, git bisect, power cut/battery, center a div, password reset TODO, force push merge conflict, AI bill from "think harder".
R8. One location, filmable with the limits above.

DELIVERABLE: exactly 3 complete stories. For each:
- TITLE
- HOOK (line 1)
- WHY IT SPREADS: the single human feeling it hits, and who will tag whom (one line each)
- FULL SCRIPT: numbered lines "CLEO: ..." / "CLAUDE: ...", plus [bracketed one-line visual notes] only where a screen/phone/visual beat matters.
- FINAL FRAME: what is on screen in the last second.
- CAPTION IDEA (one line) and the COMMENT BAIT question.
- SELF-SCORE 1-10 on: hook, retention, twist, shareability. Be harsh. If any score is below 8, rewrite the story before answering until it is 8+.
Then: which ONE of your three you would bet the channel on, and why, in two lines.
Write in plain text. No preamble.

